      "A risk term that uses only `(mean - prior)²` ignores predictive uncertainty. Two candidates with identical means but different variances rank equal under bias-only risk — which is wrong: the high-variance candidate carries higher operational risk of missing preference even though it has the same expected outcome.",
      :then
      "Compute the risk term per institution (or per state-instance) as:\n      `risk[i] = (mean-D[i] - prior-D)² + (mean-A[i] - prior-A)² + var-D[i] + var-A[i]`\n    Both the squared-deviation (bias²) and the predictive variance contribute additively to the per-instance risk. Average over instances in the band for the band-level risk-term. Configurable priors per opts."},
