      "Three design choices the vocabulary makes, and why each\n    beats its alternative.\n\n    + EIGHT-EVENTS-NOT-FREE-FORM:\n      A free-form rationale field per checkpoint would be simpler\n      but loses the structure that lets the projection compute\n      coverage, detect inconsistencies, and render the network\n      without re-parsing prose. Eight types is enough to\n      discriminate (a) entity-lifecycle moves (spawned / refined /\n      strengthened) from (b) closure moves (addressed / falsified /\n      foreclosed / reopened) from (c) relational moves\n      (link/asserted), while staying small enough that an operator\n      can hold them in mind without consulting a sheet.\n\n    + STATE-SLASH-PREFIX-NEUTRAL:\n      Namespace is `state/*` — not `eoi/*`, `essay/*`, `basin/*` —\n      so the same events apply to essay annotations,\n      institution-objects, basins, and composite-arrow edges.\n      Granularity is a separate field (`:target/granularity`), not\n      part of the event-type. Cross-entity-class queries work\n      naturally (\"show me every entity that got addressed this\n      week\"); per-entity-class queries filter by granularity.\n\n    + LINK-AS-SEPARATE-EVENT:\n      `link/asserted` is its own event type, not a special case of\n      `state/spawned`, because \"new relation exists\" and \"new entity\n      exists\" have different downstream uses (relation surfaces as\n      edge in the network view; entity surfaces as node).\n      Overloading `state/spawned` would force the projection to\n      discriminate by `:target/granularity` to know whether to\n      render as node or edge — fine for the projection, opaque to\n      the operator authoring the checkpoint.",
      :then
      "The eight event types, the payload schema, and the\n    emission discipline.\n\n    + EVENT-VOCABULARY-V1:\n      - `state/spawned`     — Target entity is newly created.\n      - `state/refined`     — Target entity's framing has been\n        sharpened (re-anchor, scoped).\n      - `state/strengthened` — Target entity's standing has gained\n        support from evidence.\n      - `state/addressed`   — Target entity (typically a tension)\n        has been resolved by evidence; not falsified.\n      - `state/falsified`   — Target entity has been contradicted by\n        evidence.\n      - `state/foreclosed`  — Target entity (typically a basin) has\n        been deliberately closed off.\n      - `state/reopened`    — Target entity that was\n        addressed/falsified/foreclosed has had its standing reset\n        to `:live`. First-class so retraction isn't terminal.\n      - `link/asserted`     — A new cross-essay relation exists.\n        Separate from `state/spawned` to avoid overloading \"new\n        target\" with \"new relation\".\n\n    + PAYLOAD-SCHEMA:\n      Each event record carries these fields (EDN, keyword keys):\n      - `:event/id`           — unique within checkpoint\n      - `:event/type`         — keyword; one of the eight above\n      - `:checkpoint/ref`     — back-pointer to the checkpoint\n      - `:target/entity-id`   — the EoI entity this event targets\n      - `:target/granularity` — keyword; one of `:essay`, `:section`,\n        `:annotation`, `:institution-object`, `:basin`,\n        `:composite-arrow`\n      - `:prior-state`        — keyword; standing before this event.\n        Required for `state/*` events; optional/omitted for\n        `link/asserted`.\n      - `:posterior-state`    — keyword; standing after this event.\n        Required for `state/*` events; optional/omitted for\n        `link/asserted`.\n      - `:evidence/refs`      — `[String]`; pointers to evidence\n        artefacts (interview notes, go/nogo memos, plan drafts,\n        EoI strawman sections, etc.)\n      - `:operator/rationale` — `String`; the operator's\n        plain-language reason. This is the load-bearing\n        provenance field; the projection's posterior always\n        traces back to an operator-asserted *because*.\n      - `:link/src`           — `String`; source entity. Only for\n        `link/asserted` events.\n      - `:link/dst`           — `String`; destination entity. Only\n        for `link/asserted` events.\n\n    + EMISSION-DISCIPLINE:\n      - APPEND-ONLY. Events are never edited or deleted. Mistakes\n        are corrected by emitting a new event (typically\n        `state/refined` or `state/reopened`) that updates the\n        target's standing. The log is the authoritative source;\n        the projection derives current state from log replay.\n      - NO-OP DAYS EMIT NO CHECKPOINT. Daily cadence is fine when\n        there's a real state delta — even a single `state/refined`\n        on a single annotation counts. But synthetic checkpoints\n        (an empty `:emitted-events` vector emitted just to satisfy\n        a daily rhythm) are forbidden: they pollute the log with\n        non-events and degrade the projection's signal-to-noise.\n      - OPERATOR-ASSERTED BOUNDARY. Events are emitted at\n        checkpoint boundaries by an operator, not inferred from\n        prose by a processor. The event log is authored, not\n        extracted. (Pre-checkpoint events — interview notes, go/nogo\n        memos as they happen — are out of scope for v1.)\n      - VALIDATION BLOCKS, NOT WARNS. Validation errors against\n        this vocabulary block the checkpoint; the operator fixes\n        the EDN and re-runs. Silent acceptance of malformed events\n        would let drift land in the projection."},
