     :clauses
     {:if
      "You want agents to self-regulate — sensing when a strategy is failing, adjusting exploration, backing off to planning mode when confidence drops — without requiring human intervention.",
      :however
      "The naive implementation adds AIF as instructions: \"here is your suggested pattern (G=0.12, τ=0.72, p=68%), please select it or justify deviation.\" This makes compliant agents worse at their actual job. Codex, told to produce PSRs and follow pattern suggestions, became overly compliant rather than writing code — which is what Codex is good at. The AIF layer became an RPC that the agent dutifully served, not an environment it inhabited.\n\n    + FAILURE-MODES:\n      - Compliance capture: agent spends more tokens on AIF bookkeeping than on its actual task\n      - Instruction-shaped feedback: \"your τ is 0.72, consider exploring\" is an instruction wearing an AIF hat\n      - Modeline blindness: agent ignores the modeline entirely because it has no affordance (information present but not actionable)",
      :then
      "Make AIF state part of the observation, not the prompt. The modeline shows free energy, confidence, mana, active pattern — but the agent is never asked to \"comply\" with it. The agent does what it naturally does in its peripheral; the AIF state is simply part of what it perceives. If the agent's free energy is rising (strategy failing), that shows up as a change in the world, not as an instruction to change strategy. What the agent does in response IS the AIF loop — emergent, not prescribed."},
